ranked_moves.what:
Syzygy extracts every checkable assertion from the project's own prose documents (README, docs/, manuals, man pages, the configuration file's comments): inline-code identifiers, configuration keys with a stated default, command or flag names, file paths, and version numbers. It checks each against the pinned tree: does the identifier exist, does the key exist with that default literal, is the command or flag defined, does the path resolve. Every row falls into one of five classes:
- resolves (the name exists; no value was compared);
- agrees (a stated value was compared and matched);
- disagrees: both sides Observed, with the document line and the code line;
- document only: named in the docs, absent from the code;
- code only: a configuration key, command or flag defined in code but in no document.
The dossier gets a 'Docs and code' reference page with counts per class. Disagreements are surfaced as callouts on the Pole that covers them. The author may explain a disagreement (renamed, deprecated, platform-specific) but never hide one. The matching is lexical, its rules are printed, and anything it cannot decide is Unknown. Nothing is judged by a model. A row is 'resolves' when the documented name exists in the tree and 'agrees' only when a stated value was also compared and matched; existence is never reported as agreement. Precision is measured as well as recall, on fixtures with planted true and false rows, and a row class whose measured precision is below a stated bound is labelled Inferred, not Observed.

